{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785649402,"nonce":"eDsUhUbHE5lultLxRflA1kb/gvc=","title":"From a 2026-08-02 survey of Longhorn / Rook-Ceph / LINSTOR / Piraeus / TrueNAS against ADR-0062. Conclusion of that survey: none of them change feed DELIVERY (they provide storage; providing storage is not composing a mount — the trichotomy holds), so stage 2a→2b is unchanged. But one thing the survey found DOES contradict a claim ADR-0062 states as decided.","message":"**The claim to revisit.** ADR-0062's privilege-ladder asserts that an unprivileged Export rung's change set is necessarily the `(size, mtime, ctime)` stat-cache approximation, and the filed-not-built \"CoW clone of the Farm\" row explicitly concedes \"that stays the stat-cache approximation\". `zfs diff` refutes the concession: it is a typed, PATH-level changelog (`+` created, `-` removed, `M` modified, `R` renamed with old→new — deletions and renames distinct), and it traverses only blocks whose birth-txg postdates the snapshot (the `zfs send -i` mechanism), so it is O(changed), not a walk. Shape-wise it is arguably BETTER than reading an overlay upper: no `trusted.overlay.*`, no redirect_dir composition, no EXDEV, and no privilege needed merely to READ the markers.\n\nSo: a ZFS (or btrfs) clone rung could be EXACT without overlayfs. btrfs is the weaker twin — `subvolume snapshot` is O(1) but `find-new` does NOT report deletions (the easy API is the silently-lossy one, exactly the failure class 0062 rejects), so the usable diff means parsing a `send -p` stream.\n\n**Why it is a ticket and not a redesign** — the costs are real and strictly more than the overlay rung already built: `zfs clone` must be MOUNTED to be used and `zfs allow mount` does not work on Linux (needs real root/CAP_SYS_ADMIN + `/dev/zfs` as a hostPath device + the host mount ns), a ZFS pool must exist on the Depot node, and we would own a `zfs diff` parser. It also does not touch delivery: a clone reaches a Step Pod only via the same three routes. **fs-level CoW is a candidate Export RUNG, not a delivery mechanism** — it competes with overlayfs+upper, never with NFS or LocalPath.\n\n- [ ] Empirical probe in the `supports_reflink()` style: `zfs diff` output shape + cost on a realistic workspace, and exactly what privileges a containerized caller needs\n- [ ] Verify overlayfs upperdir ON ZFS (needs RENAME_WHITEOUT, ~2.2) — if unsupported, choosing ZFS for `workspace.persistence` to gain reflink SILENTLY kills the overlay rung\n- [ ] Verify the `mkfs.xfs` reflink default on the target node image (decides whether an xfs storageClass actually yields the reflink rung)\n- [ ] Correct ADR-0062's two overstatements: the CoW-clone row's change-set concession, and the \"warm pool of block PVCs\" rejection (argued against EBS; materially weaker against local-first provisioners — though the block variant dies anyway on extents-not-paths)\n- [ ] Record the survey's verdicts so the next person does not re-run it (incl. Ceph RGW as a legitimate S3 cold-tier option, and TrueNAS-over-NFS being actively harmful as the Depot volume)\n\nNOTE farm.rs needs NO change: `supports_reflink()` probes empirically and any ioctl failure falls to per-file copy, so ZFS 2.2 (bclone disabled after the corruption bug) correctly reports copy and 2.3 gets the reflink rung; `FarmRung::Mixed` already models the boundary.","files":null}]}